1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:12,071
When Laurie asks Jo March to marry him, she says no, and readers have argued about it ever since.

2
00:00:12,356 --> 00:00:17,463
This guide covers the novel and a version where Jo says yes and keeps her freedom.

3
00:00:18,565 --> 00:00:22,343
Hello, and welcome to Unwritten Chapters, Study Edition.

4
00:00:22,539 --> 00:00:27,044
In the next few minutes, I'll walk you through Little Women, by Louisa May Alcott.

5
00:00:27,230 --> 00:00:31,668
It's a quick guide you can use for class, a book club, or your own curiosity.

6
00:00:31,813 --> 00:00:35,910
The full guide, with ten museum and archive sources, is linked below.

7
00:00:38,075 --> 00:00:46,354
The four March sisters, Meg, Jo, Beth and Amy, live with their mother, Marmee, while their father serves in the Civil War.

8
00:00:46,690 --> 00:00:50,821
They're poor, but their home is full of love, plays and writing.

9
00:00:51,184 --> 00:00:55,750
Next door lives Laurie, a lonely rich boy who becomes their best friend.

10
00:00:56,206 --> 00:01:00,530
Jo wants to be a writer and hates the idea of giving up her freedom.

11
00:01:00,771 --> 00:01:07,231
As they grow up, Meg marries, Beth falls ill, and Jo goes to New York to work and write.

12
00:01:07,476 --> 00:01:09,484
There she meets Professor Bhaer.

13
00:01:10,131 --> 00:01:14,403
When Laurie proposes, Jo tells him she can't love him that way.

14
00:01:14,635 --> 00:01:19,314
He later marries Amy, Beth dies, and Jo marries the professor.

15
00:01:19,510 --> 00:01:21,578
They open a school at Plumfield.

16
00:01:23,672 --> 00:01:25,168
Now for the story.

17
00:01:25,373 --> 00:01:29,424
Our sleep story for this book is called "Jo Says Yes."

18
00:01:29,612 --> 00:01:32,590
Here's how it unfolds, chapter by chapter.

19
00:01:33,028 --> 00:01:34,117
Chapter one.

20
00:01:34,286 --> 00:01:35,468
The Walk Home.

21
00:01:36,323 --> 00:01:41,539
Jo March had come home from New York in the summer, and Laurie had come home from college.

22
00:01:41,803 --> 00:01:46,055
The whole town knew what was going to happen, except maybe Jo.

23
00:01:46,557 --> 00:01:51,848
She'd gone to New York the winter before, partly to work and partly to get away.

24
00:01:52,122 --> 00:01:55,371
She'd seen it coming, the way you see weather coming.

25
00:01:55,706 --> 00:01:59,644
She'd thought that if she went away for a while, the feeling might blow over.

26
00:01:59,962 --> 00:02:04,246
She taught children in a boarding house and wrote stories for the papers at night.

27
00:02:04,482 --> 00:02:08,905
She made friends with a kind, shabby German professor who lived down the hall.

28
00:02:09,187 --> 00:02:16,683
And she came home with a little money and a great many new ideas, and the feeling was still waiting for her exactly where she'd left

29
00:02:16,683 --> 00:02:17,103
it.

30
00:02:17,518 --> 00:02:18,642
Chapter two.

31
00:02:18,790 --> 00:02:19,711
The Garret.

32
00:02:20,509 --> 00:02:27,473
They were married the next spring in the March family's little parlour, with the apple trees in blossom outside and the whole

33
00:02:27,473 --> 00:02:28,893
household crowded in.

34
00:02:29,359 --> 00:02:32,268
Meg cried and Amy arranged the flowers.

35
00:02:32,537 --> 00:02:37,723
Marmee kissed Jo on the forehead and said nothing, which was how Jo knew she was moved.

36
00:02:38,195 --> 00:02:44,697
Old Mr Laurence, Laurie's grandfather, gave Jo away in place of her father, who led the service himself.

37
00:02:45,616 --> 00:02:48,045
It was a small, plain wedding.

38
00:02:48,218 --> 00:02:50,511
Jo would have hated anything else.

39
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:55,638
Everyone expected them to live in the big Laurence house, but they didn't.

40
00:02:55,942 --> 00:03:00,437
Jo said it was too grand, and she'd always feel she ought to be dusting it.

41
00:03:00,854 --> 00:03:04,353
Instead they took a smaller house at the end of the same street.

42
00:03:04,684 --> 00:03:12,153
It had a garden and a stable, and a long, low attic under the roof that Jo claimed for her own on the very first day.

43
00:03:12,630 --> 00:03:14,882
Chapter three. Liberty.

44
00:03:15,729 --> 00:03:18,486
Jo had always been afraid of one thing.

45
00:03:18,694 --> 00:03:23,948
She'd said it out loud more than once, long before Laurie ever asked her anything.

46
00:03:24,439 --> 00:03:29,827
Liberty means freedom, being free to live your own way, and she meant it.

47
00:03:30,144 --> 00:03:35,868
She'd watched women give up their names, their money, their time and their thoughts when they married.

48
00:03:36,142 --> 00:03:42,390
She'd watched clever girls become tired wives, with no room left in their days for anything of their own.

49
00:03:42,782 --> 00:03:49,128
At fifteen, with her hair cut short and her chin in the air, she'd decided it would never happen to her.

50
00:03:50,043 --> 00:03:57,253
So in that first winter, when the snow came and the house grew quiet, she watched herself very carefully.

51
00:03:57,668 --> 00:04:01,490
It was the way you might watch a pot to see if it's going to boil over.

52
00:04:01,855 --> 00:04:05,035
She waited for the moment her liberty would start to go.

53
00:04:05,552 --> 00:04:06,760
It didn't come.

54
00:04:07,578 --> 00:04:08,978
The Last Page.

55
00:04:09,645 --> 00:04:12,399
Tonight, the professor came to visit them once, on his way to a new post in the West.

56
00:04:12,697 --> 00:04:15,250
He was as kind and shabby as ever.

57
00:04:15,381 --> 00:04:18,272
He brought Jo a book of German poems and sat by their fire all evening.

58
00:04:18,685 --> 00:04:23,598
He talked about Shakespeare with Laurie until midnight, and the next morning he went away with a basket of Marmee's apples.

59
00:04:23,978 --> 00:04:26,939
Jo was sorry to see him go.

60
00:04:27,028 --> 00:04:29,522
He'd been a good friend to her in New York, when she was lonely.

61
00:04:30,537 --> 00:04:38,719
As she watched his train pull out, she thought about how many good lives a person might live.

62
00:04:39,009 --> 00:04:43,529
The one you choose isn't the only right one.

63
00:04:43,804 --> 00:04:46,613
It's just the one that's yours.

64
00:04:46,802 --> 00:04:50,434
In this story too, Aunt March left Plumfield to Jo.

65
00:04:50,747 --> 00:04:56,214
And Jo and Laurie did exactly what anyone who knew them would have expected, and filled it.

66
00:04:58,280 --> 00:04:59,281
Jo March.

67
00:04:59,330 --> 00:05:02,367
The second sister, a fierce, funny writer.

68
00:05:03,046 --> 00:05:05,368
Theodore "Laurie" Laurence.

69
00:05:05,518 --> 00:05:06,838
The boy next door.

70
00:05:07,034 --> 00:05:08,481
Jo calls him Teddy.

71
00:05:09,142 --> 00:05:11,201
Meg, Beth and Amy.

72
00:05:11,472 --> 00:05:15,410
Jo's sisters, who are kind, gentle and artistic.

73
00:05:15,722 --> 00:05:18,542
Marmee. The girls' wise mother.

74
00:05:19,086 --> 00:05:20,316
Professor Bhaer.

75
00:05:20,552 --> 00:05:24,374
A kind, shabby German teacher Jo meets in New York.

76
00:05:26,616 --> 00:05:30,639
Liberty. Jo fears that marriage will take her freedom.

77
00:05:30,930 --> 00:05:34,387
The book and our story answer that fear differently.

78
00:05:34,742 --> 00:05:36,213
Work and art.

79
00:05:36,516 --> 00:05:42,004
Jo writes for money and for love, and must decide which stories are true to her.

80
00:05:42,859 --> 00:05:47,086
Family. The March home is poor but full of warmth.

81
00:05:47,895 --> 00:05:54,728
Choices. Our story suggests there are many good lives, and the right one is the one that's yours.

82
00:05:56,374 --> 00:05:56,625
So what's the what-if?

83
00:05:56,871 --> 00:06:00,591
Here's the turning point we changed.

84
00:06:01,238 --> 00:06:05,441
In our story, Jo says yes under the willow by the river.

85
00:06:05,689 --> 00:06:09,086
Their marriage has quarrels, but they stay best friends.

86
00:06:09,506 --> 00:06:15,645
Everything after that moment is new, but the characters still act the way their authors wrote them.

87
00:06:17,626 --> 00:06:20,699
Before we go, here's a little about who wrote it.

88
00:06:21,437 --> 00:06:28,255
Louisa May Alcott grew up in Concord, Massachusetts, the second of four sisters, just like Jo.

89
00:06:28,645 --> 00:06:34,751
Her family was often poor, and she worked as a teacher, a seamstress and a nurse in the Civil War.

90
00:06:35,149 --> 00:06:39,771
A publisher asked her to write a book for girls, and she based it on her own family.

91
00:06:40,033 --> 00:06:43,568
It was such a hit that she wrote the second part within months.

92
00:06:45,666 --> 00:06:52,168
The first part takes place during the American Civil War, while Mr March serves as an army chaplain.

93
00:06:52,549 --> 00:06:54,968
Women had few choices of work then.

94
00:06:55,301 --> 00:07:02,704
Writing for magazines was one of the few ways a woman could earn her own money, which is why Jo's stories matter so much.

95
00:07:05,105 --> 00:07:06,498
Want to see the real thing?

96
